The history of AI is best understood as a series of major paradigm shifts, rather than one continuous invention. Here is a useful timeline from the intellectual origins to today's generative and agentic AI.

Major Timeline of AI: Origins → 2026
Period	Milestone	Why it mattered
1943	McCulloch & Pitts artificial neuron	First mathematical model of a neuron; foundation of neural networks
1950	Alan Turing — Computing Machinery and Intelligence	Proposed the famous Turing Test and asked whether machines can think
1956 ⭐	Dartmouth Conference	The term "Artificial Intelligence" was formally introduced; commonly regarded as the birth of AI as a field
1957–59	Perceptron; early machine learning	Frank Rosenblatt developed the perceptron, an early trainable neural network
1960s	Symbolic AI / expert reasoning	AI focused on logic, rules, search, and symbolic representation
1966	ELIZA	One of the first famous natural-language conversational programs
1970s	First AI winter begins	High expectations collided with limited computing power and difficult problems
1980s	Expert systems boom	Rule-based systems became commercially important
1986 ⭐	Backpropagation popularized	Rumelhart, Hinton & Williams helped establish the training method that became fundamental to modern neural networks
1987–93	Second AI winter	Expert-system limitations and funding decline
1997 ⭐	IBM Deep Blue defeats Garry Kasparov	Landmark demonstration that computers could outperform humans in a complex intellectual game
2006	Deep learning revival	Hinton and others helped revive neural-network research under the "deep learning" paradigm
2007–12	Big Data + GPUs	Massive datasets and GPU computing made large neural networks practical
2011	IBM Watson wins Jeopardy!	Demonstrated large-scale natural-language question answering
2012 ⭐	AlexNet wins ImageNet	One of the biggest turning points: deep neural networks dramatically improved image recognition
2014	GANs introduced	Generative Adversarial Networks opened a new era of machine-generated images and other content
2016 ⭐	AlphaGo defeats Lee Sedol	Deep learning + reinforcement learning achieved a breakthrough in a problem previously considered extremely difficult for AI
2017 ⭐	Transformer architecture	"Attention Is All You Need" introduced the Transformer, the technological foundation of modern LLMs
2018	BERT	Transformers became extremely powerful for language understanding
2020	GPT-3	Large language models demonstrated striking general-purpose language abilities
2020	AlphaFold 2	AI achieved a major breakthrough in predicting protein structures
2022 ⭐	ChatGPT launched	Generative AI became accessible to hundreds of millions of ordinary users
2022–23	Diffusion models explode	AI-generated images became extraordinarily capable
2023	GPT-4	Major leap in reasoning, multimodality, coding, and general-purpose AI
2023–24	Multimodal AI	Models increasingly handle text + image + audio + video
2024	AI agents emerge	AI increasingly moves from "answering questions" toward using tools and completing tasks
2024–25	Reasoning models	Models increasingly use extended inference/reasoning to solve difficult mathematical, scientific, and coding problems
2025–26	Agentic + multimodal AI	AI increasingly combines reasoning, tools, memory, coding, vision, voice, and autonomous workflows
2026	Current frontier	Competition shifts toward reasoning + agents + multimodality + robotics + scientific AI
The 7 Great Eras of AI

If you want to remember the whole history, I would compress it into seven eras:

1. 1943–1956 — The Intellectual Birth

The key question was:

Can intelligence be represented mathematically and implemented by a machine?

Three foundational ideas appeared:

1943 — artificial neurons
McCulloch and Pitts modeled neurons mathematically.

1950 — Turing
Alan Turing asked whether machines could exhibit intelligent behavior.

1956 — Dartmouth
John McCarthy, Marvin Minsky, Claude Shannon and others established AI as a research field.

1956 is therefore the most commonly cited "birth year" of AI.

2. 1956–1980 — Symbolic AI

Early AI was primarily rule-based.

The basic idea was:

Intelligence = symbols + rules + logical reasoning.

Machines were taught things like:

IF condition A
AND condition B
THEN conclusion C

This produced systems capable of solving puzzles, proving theorems and performing limited reasoning.

But there was a fundamental problem:

The real world is vastly more complicated than a collection of hand-written rules.

This contributed to the first AI winter.

3. 1980–2006 — Expert Systems → Machine Learning

AI temporarily shifted toward expert systems.

Instead of trying to create a universal intelligent machine, researchers encoded the knowledge of human experts.

Examples included medical diagnosis and industrial troubleshooting.

But another problem emerged:

Humans are terrible at explicitly describing all the knowledge they unconsciously use.

This encouraged a major conceptual shift:

Instead of programming intelligence → let machines learn intelligence from data.

That is the beginning of modern machine learning.

4. 2006–2016 — Deep Learning Revolution

This is arguably the most important technological transition before today's generative AI.

Three things came together:

Big data + GPUs + neural networks

Then came the landmarks:

2012 — AlexNet

Deep learning crushed traditional computer-vision approaches on ImageNet.

That demonstrated something profound:

Give sufficiently large neural networks enough data and computation, and they can learn representations humans previously had to engineer manually.

Then:

2016 — AlphaGo

Deep learning + reinforcement learning defeated one of the world's strongest Go players.

AI had crossed another psychological barrier.

5. 2017–2021 — The Transformer Revolution

This is the most important technical turning point for today's AI.

2017 — Transformer

Google researchers published:

"Attention Is All You Need."

The Transformer architecture introduced a much more scalable way to process relationships between words/tokens.

This eventually produced:

BERT → GPT → large language models → ChatGPT

The crucial idea became:

Scale the model + scale the data + scale the computation → increasingly general capabilities.

This changed AI from specialized systems into something much closer to a general-purpose computing interface.

6. 2022–2024 — Generative AI Revolution
November 30, 2022

ChatGPT was released.

This was arguably AI's equivalent of the iPhone moment.

AI went from something primarily used by researchers and companies to something ordinary people could talk to directly.

At the same time:

GPT-4
Claude
Gemini
Stable Diffusion
Midjourney
DALL-E
GitHub Copilot

rapidly transformed:

language → images → programming → audio → video

AI was no longer merely recognizing information.

It could generate information.

7. 2024–2026 — Reasoning + Agents + Multimodal AI

This is the era we are entering now.

The important transition is:

Old AI

You ask → AI answers.

New AI

You give AI a goal → AI reasons → plans → uses tools → performs actions → checks results → continues.

That is the emergence of AI agents.

At the same time, AI is becoming increasingly:

Multimodal

